{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01UCWs6v1ghc8BPZ1gwKN3De","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand the current state and determine what to build next.\n\n## Project Analysis\n\n**What this project solves:**\ngo-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as the test bed. It derives formal specifications from C source code, generates Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verifies parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**High-level architecture:**\n- `cmd/` - Go implementation of each utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (terminal width, file metadata, signal handling)\n\n\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (columns, ANSI color, human-readable sizes)\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications, PRDs, use cases, test suites\n\n**Current implementation state:**\nLooking at the release roadmap, `ts` is in progress with code not yet started, while `wc`, `cat`, and `sponge` are pending along with the `ls` and `du` utilities across the next two releases.\n\nThe generated code actually exists at the tagged commits from the pipeline runs—about 4,500 lines across 22 files including the test utilities, command implementations, and formatting packages—but it hasn't been merged into the working branch yet, which is why the analysis shows everything as not started.\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is **rel01.0**, which covers the `ts` specification and differential testing, and that's where I need to focus next.\n\nSince the code size constraints require staying under 250 lines per task and splitting implementation from tests, I should propose a single focused task to implement the `cmd/ts` utility based on the prd004-ts specification. order: (1) types and interfaces, (2) implementation, (3) tests.\n\nBut also:\n> A single task must never both implement and test the same feature.\n> If the types and interfaces are small enough to fit within the implementation task's line budget, combine steps 1 and 2 into one task.\n\nFor cmd/ts, given the 150-250 line budget per task, I need to think about what makes sense:\n\nLooking at the PRD for ts (prd004-ts):\n- R1: Default behavior (no flags) - reading stdin, timestamping, flushing\n- R2: Custom format string - positional argument\n- R3: Incremental mode (-i)\n- R4: Elapsed-since-start mode (-s)\n- R5: Monotonic clock mode (-m)\n- R6: Relative-time conversion mode (-r)\n- R7: Exit codes and error handling\n- R8: Environment\n- R9: Differential testing\n\nThe ts utility is a Perl script in the original, and the Go implementation would need to handle main logic for reading stdin and timestamping lines, plus various mode flags. Given the constraint of proposing exactly 1 task, I need to figure out what's most valuable to tackle first. The dependency chain suggests starting with the core functionality, but I'm weighing whether to focus on the basic implementation or set up the testing infrastructure.\n\nSince the existing code lives at tags rather than on this branch and the status shows \"not started,\" implementing cmd/ts/main.go makes the most sense as the first task—it's the earliest incomplete release, ts doesn't depend on other packages like pkg/sys or pkg/format, and the PRD is complete. The Perl script is relatively straightforward, so I'm estimating around 200-250 lines for a solid implementation covering the main logic and flag handling.\n\nBut I need to reconsider the dependency chain here. The planning constitution requires splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks, so I should propose just the implementation. However, pkg/testutils also needs to be implemented since it exists only at the generation-merged tags, not on the current branch. The question is which comes first—libraries before consumers suggests pkg/testutils should come before cmd/ts, but cmd/ts implementation doesn't actually depend on pkg/testutils existing yet, only the tests do. by the tests of `cmd/ts`, not by the implementation of `cmd/ts`. The implementation of `cmd/ts` doesn't depend on `pkg/testutils`. So the dependency chain is: cmd/ts implementation first, then pkg/testutils, then cmd/ts tests.\n\nThe most valuable single task would be the `cmd/ts` implementation since it directly maps to the use case, but that creates a problem—without pkg/testutils, I can't write the tests needed to verify it works. Yet the constraint limits me to proposing just one task. Since all specifications are complete and no code exists on the working branch yet, I need to pick whichever task unblocks the others most effectively. The logical first task is `pkg/testutils` implementation since it's a dependency for tests. But cmd/ts can be implemented without it. \n\nGiven only 1 task allowed, I should pick the task that unblocks the most subsequent work. pkg/testutils enables all the test suites (cmd/ts, cmd/wc, cmd/cat, cmd/sponge), while cmd/ts only enables its own tests. Following the planning constitution—libraries and shared packages before consumers—pkg/testutils should come first.\n\nLooking at the PRD requirements, I need to start with the foundational types and interfaces: the DiffTest struct and Normalize function type, which everything else builds on. - but this might be combined with implementation if small enough\n2. Implementation (RunDiffTests, execution, comparison)\n3. Tests\n\nFor a single task, given the 150-250 line budget, I should probably propose the full pkg/testutils implementation (types + core harness) since it's foundational. But that might exceed 250 lines.\n\nLet me estimate:\n- DiffTest struct definition: ~20 lines\n- Normalize type: ~5 lines\n- RunDiffTests function: ~80-100 lines\n- Binary execution helper: ~50-60 lines\n- Comparison and reporting: ~40-50 lines\n- TimestampNormalizer: ~20-30 lines\n- File-state comparison: ~30-40 lines\n\nTotal estimate: ~250-300 lines. That's tight but feasible for one task if I focus on the essentials.\n\nSince the types are simple enough to fit within the line budget, I can combine the type definitions with the implementation into a single task. Looking at the requirements, prd001 has exactly 5 requirement groups (R1-R5), which aligns well with the 5-8 requirement target for code tasks. I'll structure this as one comprehensive task covering the full differential testing harness implementation. I'm checking the naming convention against the planning constitution—the primary file should be named after the primary exported type, so `difftest.go` for DiffTest and `normalizer.go` for TimestampNormalizer rather than a generic `testutils.go`. Now I need to work through the design decisions that flow from the PRD requirements. prd001:\n- R1.1: DiffTest struct fields: Args []string, Stdin []byte, Env []string, ExitCode int, Normalize []Normalize (slice per R4.3)\n- R1.4: Normalize function type: func([]byte) []byte\n- R2.3: Default timeout is 10 seconds\n- R2.5: Per-test temp directory created by harness when not specified\n- R3.6: Each DiffTest runs as t.Run subtest\n- R4.2: Built-in TimestampNormalizer for ts tests\n- R4.3: Normalize is a slice of functions applied in order\n\nSo the DiffTest.Normalize field holds a slice of normalizer functions that get applied sequentially to the output, with an empty slice meaning no normalization happens. For the acceptance criteria, I need to verify that the cmd/ package can invoke RunDiffTests with the binary paths and test slice, that failures show the relevant details, that TimestampNormalizer correctly replaces timestamps with a placeholder, and that file content mismatches are properly detected.\n\nI should also check AC5 and AC6 - timeout failures need clear messaging and the code must pass linting. Since the planning constitution says to split implementation and tests into separate tasks, I won't include the test file itself in this task, just the implementation of the harness.\n\nNow I'm laying out the complete task structure with the files to create and the specific requirements from the PRD, including the DiffTest struct definition, normalizer composition helpers, and the entry point for running differential tests. , applying the Normalize functions to both binaries' outputs before comparing them byte-for-byte, then reporting any divergence with the test arguments, truncated stdin, both stdouts and stderrs, and exit codes, all wrapped in a named subtest. For normalization, I need to implement a TimestampNormalizer that converts common timestamp patterns like \"Feb 19 12:34:56\" into a fixed placeholder so timing differences don't cause false failures.\n\nNow I'm extending DiffTest to handle file-based outputs: adding a FileExpectations field that holds a slice of FileExpectation structs, each specifying a file path and expected content. After both binaries finish, I'll read each expected file and compare its content byte-for-byte against what was expected, reporting any mismatches in the same format as stdout divergence.\n\nThe RunDiffTests signature matches the acceptance criteria, and the Normalize field is a slice of functions applied sequentially to both outputs before comparison. I'm using os/exec with context timeouts (10-second default) for binary invocation, and the TimestampNormalizer will match the strftime format used by ts and replace matched timestamps with a consistent literal. string 'TIMESTAMP'. Use a regexp.MustCompile pattern for the replacement.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"A caller can define []DiffTest and invoke RunDiffTests(t, goBinaryPath, refBinaryPath, tests) with no other setup, per prd001 AC1.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"A passing DiffTest run produces no test output. A failing run prints the triggering args, stdin (truncated to 256 bytes if longer), and both binaries' stdout, stderr, and exit code, per prd001 AC2.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer replaces a string matching the ts default timestamp format (e.g. 'Feb 19 14:05:32') with 'TIMESTAMP' so that ts differential tests pass despite wall-clock differences, per prd001 AC3.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"A DiffTest with a FileExpectation whose expected content differs from what the Go binary writes causes a test failure with the path and both file contents reported, per prd001 AC4.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"A DiffTest that exceeds the 10-second timeout fails with a clear timeout message rather than hanging the test run, per prd001 AC5.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"pkg/testutils compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint and passes go build ./pkg/testutils/..., per prd001 AC6.\"\n\nNow I'm estimating the implementation scope—difftest.go will need the DiffTest struct, NormalizeFunc type, RunDiffTests function with binary execution and comparison logic, plus the timeout handling and file validation pieces. I'm verifying the module path matches the go.mod declaration and confirming the task description is complete enough for implementation. Now I'll finalize the YAML output with careful attention to formatting.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":73595,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":73595},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"06f1b2b6-510e-4030-9991-1e76202f49cc","uuid":"accab983-4e40-4341-8867-6a5617d2177c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01UCWs6v1ghc8BPZ1gwKN3De","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils experiments with specification-driven code generation using GNU Unix utilities as the test bed. It derives formal specs from C source, generates Go implementations via a Claude orchestrator, and verifies correctness through differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:**\n- `pkg/testutils/` — differential testing harness (shared by all cmd/ tests)\n- `pkg/sys/` — portable syscall abstractions (terminal width, file metadata, signal handling)\n- `pkg/format/` — shared output formatting (columns, ANSI color, human-readable sizes)\n- `cmd/<utility>/` — one package per Unix utility\n- `magefiles/` — build/lint/generation targets\n\n**Current state:** All PRDs, use cases, and test suites are authored. Generated Go code (pkg/testutils, cmd/ts, cmd/cat, cmd/wc, cmd/sponge, pkg/sys, pkg/format) exists only at generation-merged tags — not on the working branch. All use cases show code status \"not started\" on this branch.\n\n**Current release:** rel01.0 (Batch 1 — ts), `in_progress`. The single use case `rel01.0-uc001-ts` is in progress. Before cmd/ts tests can run, `pkg/testutils` must exist (it is a dependency of every differential test suite).\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nDependency order: `pkg/testutils` → `cmd/ts` implementation → `cmd/ts` tests. pkg/testutils is consumed by every test suite in the project (rel01.0 through rel02.1). Implementing it first unblocks all subsequent test tasks. It maps to prd001-testutils, which is fully specified with 5 requirement groups, 6 acceptance criteria, and a well-defined API surface.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement pkg/testutils differential testing harness (prd001 R1-R5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/testutils component section)\n      - go.mod (module path for import declarations)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/testutils/difftest.go\n        action: create\n        note: DiffTest struct, NormalizeFunc type, RunDiffTests entry point, binary execution, comparison and reporting, file-state comparison\n      - path: pkg/testutils/normalizer.go\n        action: create\n        note: TimestampNormalizer built-in normalizer\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Define the DiffTest struct per prd001 R1.1-R1.4 with fields Args []string, Stdin []byte, Env []string, ExitCode int, Normalizers []NormalizeFunc, FileExpectations []FileExpectation, and WDir string. Define NormalizeFunc as func([]byte) []byte. Define FileExpectation as a struct with Path string and Content []byte. Nil Stdin means immediate EOF; nil Env means the binary inherits the test process environment; nil or empty Normalizers means no normalization is applied.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement binary execution per prd001 R2.1-R2.5: RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary string, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) invokes both binaries with identical Args, Stdin, and Env, captures stdout/stderr/exit code from each independently, imposes a 10-second default timeout (fail with clear timeout message if exceeded), and uses a per-test os.MkdirTemp working directory when DiffTest.WDir is empty.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement comparison and reporting per prd001 R3.1-R3.6: apply DiffTest.Normalizers in order to both binaries' stdout and stderr before comparison; compare normalized stdout byte-for-byte, normalized stderr byte-for-byte, and exit codes exactly; report divergence with triggering args, stdin truncated to 256 bytes, both stdouts, both stderrs, and both exit codes; run each DiffTest as a t.Run named subtest using the DiffTest index or a name derived from its args.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement normalization composition per prd001 R4.1-R4.3: the Normalizers slice on DiffTest is applied left-to-right to both binaries' outputs before comparison. Implement TimestampNormalizer in normalizer.go as a NormalizeFunc that replaces strings matching the ts default strftime format '%b %d %H:%M:%S' (e.g. 'Feb 19 14:05:32') with the fixed placeholder 'TIMESTAMP' using a compiled regexp.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Implement file-state comparison per prd001 R5.1-R5.2: after both binaries exit, for each FileExpectation in DiffTest.FileExpectations read the file at Path (relative to the test working directory used by that binary's invocation) and compare its content byte-for-byte with FileExpectation.Content, reporting path and both file contents on divergence using the same t.Errorf format as stdout divergence.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Name the primary file difftest.go because the primary exported type is DiffTest, per the project file naming convention (testutils/testutils.go is forbidden).\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"RunDiffTests signature is RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary string, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) per prd001 AC1. The function is the sole public entry point for the harness.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"The Normalizers field on DiffTest is []NormalizeFunc (a slice, not a single function) per prd001 R4.3. Both binaries' outputs are passed through the same normalizer sequence before byte-for-byte comparison.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Use exec.CommandContext with context.WithTimeout for binary invocation per prd001 R2.3. Set the default timeout to 10 seconds via a package-level constant DefaultTimeout = 10 * time.Second. Do not expose a per-test timeout override in this task.\"\n      - id: D5\n        text: \"Each binary invocation runs in its own subdirectory under the per-test temp directory so that file-state comparisons (prd001 R5) read files written by the correct binary without cross-contamination.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"A caller can define []DiffTest and invoke RunDiffTests(t, goBinaryPath, refBinaryPath, tests) with no other setup, per prd001 AC1.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"A passing DiffTest run emits no test output. A failing run prints the triggering args, stdin (truncated to 256 bytes), and both binaries' stdout, stderr, and exit code, per prd001 AC2.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"TimestampNormalizer replaces a string matching the ts default format (e.g. 'Feb 19 14:05:32') with 'TIMESTAMP' so that ts differential tests pass despite wall-clock differences, per prd001 AC3.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"A DiffTest with a FileExpectation whose expected content differs from what one binary writes causes a test failure reporting the path and both file contents, per prd001 AC4.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"A DiffTest whose binary exceeds 10 seconds fails immediately with a clear timeout message and does not hang the test run, per prd001 AC5.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"go build ./pkg/testutils/... succeeds and golangci-lint reports no warnings on pkg/testutils/, per prd001 AC6.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":73595,"cache_read_input_tokens":15559,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":73595},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"06f1b2b6-510e-4030-9991-1e76202f49cc","uuid":"cabb095d-6eac-458b-a195-30bf3e37d330"}
